in @prism

in @cogito
in @epistemologic/reality/time
in @epistemologic/reality/silicon/compute_bound
in @epistemologic/property/autopoietic

# @epistemologic/property/halts
#
# A type halts iff every reflexive trajectory of its tick map
# terminates in bounded steps. Per Alex's 2026-05-25 substrate
# decision, termination has TWO conditions joined by disjunction:
#
#   (a) AUTOPOIETIC SETTLEMENT.
#       reflect(τ.state_n) == fixed_point. The trajectory reaches a
#       Lawvere fixed point of @cogito.reflect (the observe |>
#       strategy |> perturb tick). Per Soto-Andrade & Varela 1984:
#       this is equivalent to the reflect loop being autopoietic.
#       Per @cogito.autopoietic: the property that names this
#       structural condition.
#
#   (b) REDUCTION EXHAUSTION.
#       reductions(τ.steps_0..n) >= reduction_budget(shard). The
#       trajectory consumed the budget allocated by the load
#       topology (the host shard's @epistemologic/silicon/
#       compute_bound.max_reductions, weighted by current load via
#       the Scheduler Tower's KMS temperature β). When exhaustion
#       fires, the substrate forces a halt; the gen_prism either
#       crystallises a partial result or returns imperfect.
#
# Either condition suffices. The disjunction is decidable because:
#
#   - the trajectory τ is sub-Turing by construction (mirror's
#     grammar class refuses unbounded reachable state spectra; see
#     docs/specs/is-copium.md sections "Sub-Turing Grammar as
#     Structural Escape" and "¬is_copium(mirror) — by construction");
#   - @cogito.reflect is structurally computable on every tick;
#   - reductions are countable per tick at the substrate;
#   - reduction_budget(shard) is computable from a load-topology
#     snapshot via @epistemologic/silicon/compute_bound.
#
# This is the is_copium thesis operationalized. Per docs/specs/
# is-copium.md: AI alignment on Turing-complete substrates is
# undecidable by Rice's theorem (1951). Mirror's sub-Turing escape
# + this property = decidable termination by construction. Every
# grammar that compiles, and every gen_prism that ticks inside one,
# is proven-to-halt.
#
# Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-gram-and-mirror-same-architecture-
# two-altitudes.md: GRAM's deep-supervision recursion + adaptive
# computation time matches mirror's gen_prism ancestor chain +
# reduction-budget exhaustion at the structural altitude. The halt
# property is the substrate-level analog of GRAM's ACT (Adaptive
# Computation Time): a structural budget that bounds inner
# recursion when the autopoietic fixed point is not yet reached.
#
# Sibling properties: autopoietic(T) (clause (a) reduces to this on
# the tick map), monotonicity(T) (tick deltas and the reduction
# counter are both @time.monotonic-typed; non-decreasing is what
# makes the budget comparison well-defined), causality(T) (the
# trajectory's ancestor chain IS the causal DAG the budget walks).
#
# Spec: docs/specs/is-copium.md, docs/specs/scheduler-tower.md.

grammar @epistemologic/property/halts {

  # Clause (a): the autopoietic settlement check. The reflect loop
  # reaches a Lawvere fixed point in bounded steps. Delegates to
  # @epistemologic/property/autopoietic.autopoietic applied to the
  # type's tick map. Per Soto-Andrade & Varela 1984: autopoiesis IS
  # the existence of a Lawvere fixed point on the reflexive map.
  # Verifier: the same decidable check autopoietic uses (Banach
  # contraction on hash space, exhibited at construction time).
  autopoietic_settles(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # Clause (b): the reduction-exhaustion check. The trajectory
  # consumes at most reduction_budget(shard) reductions before the
  # substrate forces a halt. Verifier walks the type's tick map,
  # counts the reductions per tick (countable at the substrate),
  # and confirms the bound holds against the budget read from
  # @epistemologic/silicon/compute_bound.max_reductions on the host
  # shard. The budget is a @time.monotonic value when expressed as
  # wall-time-equivalent; for raw reduction counts it is a u64.
  reductions_bounded(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The disjunction is decidable. Both clauses are structurally
  # computable; their union is the halting condition. This verifier
  # confirms that AT LEAST ONE of (a) or (b) fires for every
  # reachable trajectory of the type. Sub-Turing construction
  # guarantees the reachable set is enumerable; the verifier walks
  # it once. No oracle needed; Rice's theorem does not apply
  # because the language is not Turing-complete.
  disjunction_decidable(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The combined property: the type halts.
  # Pass iff (autopoietic_settles or reductions_bounded) and
  # disjunction_decidable. The substrate exhibits the witness at
  # construction; the property records the structural contract.
  halts(type) -> verdict { \ }
}

out autopoietic_settles
out reductions_bounded
out disjunction_decidable
out halts
out @epistemologic/property/halts
